## Migrating from `origin`

Earlier 2.0.0 release candidates also accepted `origin`. The API turned it into a hidden origin group named `Origins for <cname> (<resource id>)`. Terraform did not manage that group, so it stayed behind after `terraform destroy`. After upgrading, replace `origin` with `origin_group` and import the existing group, so it is managed and deleted with the CDN resource. The group's ID is already in state as `origin_group` (see `terraform state show`).

```terraform
import {
  to = gcore_cdn_origin_group.example
  id = "<origin_group id from state>"
}

resource "gcore_cdn_origin_group" "example" {
  name    = "Origins for cdn.example.com (<resource id>)"
  sources = [{ source = "example.com" }]
}

resource "gcore_cdn_resource" "example" {
  cname        = "cdn.example.com"
  origin_group = gcore_cdn_origin_group.example.id
}
```

The plan should show one import and no changes to the CDN resource.

## Turning options off

Removing an option from `options`, or removing the whole `options` block, does not turn the option off. The CDN resource keeps the option's current setting, and the plan shows no changes. To turn an option off, keep it in the configuration and set `enabled = false`:

```terraform
resource "gcore_cdn_resource" "example" {
  cname        = "cdn.example.com"
  origin_group = gcore_cdn_origin_group.example.id

  options = {
    gzip_on = {
      enabled = false
      value   = true
    }
  }
}
```

The same applies to `custom_values` in `edge_cache_settings`. Removing it keeps the current per-status-code values, including when you switch from `value` to `default`. Set `custom_values = {}` to clear them.

